# KTC cache. directory.json is today's snapshot; players/<name_id>.json
# is per-player full history (back to 2021). Reproducible from dynasty-daddy
# and accumulates to ~1k files — not worth committing.
data/ktc_cache/

# Python bytecode caches
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Built every run and uploaded with the LOTG_outputs artifact, but NOT committed.
# It is a zip of the CSVs sitting beside it — the same bytes twice — and being
# already-compressed, git can neither zlib nor delta it: every rebuild stored a
# fresh ~9.4 MB blob. Measured 2026-09-08, it was 122 MB of a 210 MB repo, 58% of
# everything, for data the repo already had. The CSVs are the source of truth;
# grab the zip from the run artifact when you want one file.
exports/LOTG_Exports.zip

# The other half of the same bill. An .xlsx IS a zip, so git can neither zlib nor
# delta this one either: 40 versions cost 273 MB of on-disk history — 32% of
# everything reachable from main — at ~6.8 MB per rebuild. Every NUMBER in it is
# in the CSVs beside it; what is workbook-only is presentation (tab order,
# conditional formatting, the hover-comment histories, the PH# hyperlinks). Built
# every run and uploaded with the LOTG_outputs artifact — grab it there.
#
# Two readers take a workbook when one is on disk: scripts/audit_player_history.py
# and tests/test_player_history_continuity.py. CI builds one before pytest, so both
# still run there exactly as before. Against a bare checkout the test now SKIPS
# instead of auditing the committed copy — which is the honest outcome, since that
# copy was a build artifact of whatever run last refreshed it.
exports/LOTG_Stats.xlsx

# nflverse SEASON totals, pulled on demand by `lotg_support.scoring_events` for
# career questions. Inquiry-only: the build never reads them, they are not part
# of the committed cold-start baseline, and there are 27 of them.
.cache/seasons/
